    "## 15. Conclusions and Limits\n",
"\n",
"**What I learned from this project:**\n",
"\n",
"The main factors linked to customer churn are the contract type and the time spent with the company (tenure). Customers with a month-to-month contract, less than one year of tenure, and a high monthly bill are more likely to leave.\n",
"\n",
"If I were advising the company, I would first target these customers with offers to switch to a longer contract. I would also look at how the support team treats customers, as staff attitude is one of the common reasons for leaving.\n",
"\n",
"This project also taught me that accuracy alone is not enough to evaluate a model. The test set must not be used before the final evaluation. I also learned that filtering data can create bias, as shown by the 100% churn rate among customers with very short tenure.\n",
"\n",
"These recommendations are only ideas to test. They are not proven causes of churn. The company should use an A/B test to check if these offers really work.\n",
"\n",
"**Limits**\n",
"\n",
"- The dataset is fictional, and the costs are estimated. In a real situation, the success of an offer would need to be tested with an A/B test.\n",
"- The model only works for customers with more than 3 months of tenure.\n",
"- The threshold was chosen using the training data, so the profit estimate may be slightly too high. The test set is also small, so the results may change.\n",
"- SHAP helps explain the model's predictions, but it does not explain the real causes of churn. The reasons given by customers only come from people who have already left.\n",
"- `Satisfaction Score` and `CLTV` were not included as a precaution, and I did not test their impact.\n",
"\n",
"**A mistake I fixed:**\n",
"\n",
"At first, my filter removed customers who had just joined, but kept customers who had left very recently. As a result, the data showed that 100% of customers with 3 months of tenure or less had left. This made the model look better than it really was.\n",
"\n",
"I first noticed this problem on the SHAP chart, where some points were far away from the others. I then checked the tenure table in section 4 and confirmed the mistake."
